goawk is the one component this toolchain builds from Go source, so it cannot link the module the Rust
components share (`patches/toolchain/path-alias/dsh_path_alias.rs`); `dsh_path_alias.go` is its twin,
`scripts/build-toolchain.mjs` injects it into this checkout, and this patch is where it is applied. The
rule has to be applied inside the program because the engine only rewrites the command path and the
arguments **it** spawns: a program that another program starts — the child of `xargs` or `find -exec` —
gets its arguments from that program, so `printf '/tmp/alias-a.txt\n' | xargs awk '{print}'` handed `awk`
a literal `/tmp/alias-a.txt`, which Windows resolves against the current drive's root and reports as a
missing file. `alias-chain-xargs-awk` in `corpus/compat.json` pins exactly that chain, and was a
`knownGap` until this patch landed; the other programs in the chain (`rm`, `grep`, `sed`, `jq`) already
carried the rule. `argv-semantics` pins the same rule for a directly spawned program, which is what keeps
this file and the Rust twin in step.

Arguments are translated as they are read, before the manual parse below looks at them, so every operand
this program handles — the program text, the input files, a `-f progfile` value — is one boundary rather
than one per flag.

diff --git a/goawk.go b/goawk.go
--- a/goawk.go
+++ b/goawk.go
@@ -83,6 +83,11 @@ GoAWK debugging arguments:
 )
 
 func main() {
+	// A program that another program starts — the child of `xargs` or `find -exec` — gets its arguments
+	// from that program rather than from the shell, which is where the engine's own rewrite cannot
+	// reach. `dsh_path_alias.go` applies the same `/tmp` and `/<letter>` rule to the arguments here.
+	dshTranslateArguments(os.Args[1:], os.TempDir)
+
 	// Parse command line arguments manually rather than using the
 	// "flag" package, so we can support flags with no space between
 	// flag and argument, like '-F:' (allowed by POSIX)
